.\" Manual page for krtek. Keep it in step with the usage text in
.\" src/tui/main.zig and with the key map behind ? in the app.
.TH KRTEK 1 "August 2026" "krtek" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
krtek \- a database manager for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B krtek
.RI [ database.db ]
.br
.B krtek
.RI postgres://user@host:port/database
.br
.B krtek
.RI mysql://user@host:port/database
.br
.B krtek
.RI redis://host:port/index
.br
.B krtek
.RI kafka://host:port
.br
.B krtek
.RI kafka+ssl://user@host:port?mechanism=SCRAM-SHA-256
.br
.B krtek
.RI s3://bucket
.br
.B krtek
.RI s3+http://key:secret@localhost:9000/bucket
.br
.B krtek
.RI azure://account:key@container
.br
.B krtek
.RI rabbit://guest@host:15672/vhost
.br
.B krtek
.RI sftp://user@host/srv/data
.br
.B krtek
.RI k8s://context/namespace
.br
.B krtek
.RB \(dq host=...\ dbname=...\ user=... \(dq
.br
.B krtek
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B krtek
.RB [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B krtek
browses, edits, alters, dumps and imports a database on a text screen. It speaks
SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB, Redis, Kafka, S3, Azure Blob, RabbitMQ, SFTP
and Kubernetes behind one interface, and needs nothing installed: the client
libraries are linked into the binary, and the rest are spoken directly.
.PP
A Kafka topic is a table of
.IR partition ", " offset ", " timestamp ", " key ", " value " and " headers .
Records cannot be changed - the log is append-only - but they can be written, and
the editor is a Kafka console:
.BR TOPICS ", " OFFSETS ", " DESCRIBE ", " CREATE ", " DROP ", " TRUNCATE ", " PRODUCE .
TLS and SASL (PLAIN, SCRAM-SHA-256, SCRAM-SHA-512) are asked for in the target:
.IR kafka+ssl://user@host:9093?mechanism=SCRAM-SHA-256 .
.PP
An S3 bucket is a table of
.IR key ", " size ", " modified ", " etag " and " storage ,
listed a page at a time by continuation token and filtered by key prefix. The
editor is an S3 console:
.BR BUCKETS ", " LS ", " GET ", " HEAD ", " PUT ", " DEL ", " URL .
Credentials come from the target, from
.BR AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID " and friends, or from " ~/.aws/credentials ,
in that order. A target that names a key and no secret is asked for the secret,
which is kept wherever that connection keeps its password. Anything that speaks
S3 works:
.IR s3+http://key:secret@localhost:9000/bucket .
.PP
Azure Blob is the same shape with a signature of its own: a container is a table
and a blob is a row. A target is
.IR azure://account:key@container ,
an endpoint of its own for the emulator or a proxy, or the connection string the
portal hands out, pasted as it stands. The account key can be typed as the
password instead, or left in
.BR AZURE_STORAGE_KEY ;
a shared access signature works in place of one.
.PP
RabbitMQ is reached through its management API, and what is a table is the
topology:
.IR queues ", " exchanges ", " bindings ", " consumers ", " connections ", " channels " and " nodes ,
with a vhost for a schema.
.B Reading a queue is destructive
- AMQP has no peek - so the interface never touches messages; the console does,
and says which of the two it did:
.B PEEK
puts them back, which changes their order and marks them redelivered, and
.B DRAIN
keeps them off. The rest of the console is
.BR PUBLISH ", " PURGE ", " DECLARE ", " BIND ", " DELETE " and " CLOSE .
An
.I amqp://
url is taken as well; its port is not, because 5672 does not answer HTTP.
.PP
Kubernetes maps a resource kind to a table and a namespace to a schema:
.IR pods ", " deployments ", " services ", " nodes " and fourteen others,"
with the columns kubectl prints, ages and ready counts included. A target names a
context and at most a namespace -
.IR k8s://prod/payments ,
or nothing at all for the kubeconfig's current context - because everything else
is in the kubeconfig, which is read the way kubectl reads it: its certificate
authority and client certificate, and
.B exec
credential plugins, which are run without a shell and given half a minute. Objects
are read, deleted and scaled, and not edited: one is a document with a controller
acting on it, and writing it back from a grid would lose every field the grid does
not show.
.B x
asks before deleting, because nothing takes that back. The console is
.BR GET ", " DESCRIBE ", " LOGS ", " SCALE ", " RESTART ", " USE ", " NAMESPACES ", " CONTEXTS " and " VERSION .
.PP
SFTP is a filesystem rather than a store of keys: a directory is a table and a
file is a row, a directory is also what a schema is - so
.B #
walks the tree - and a rename is a real rename. The editor is a small shell:
.BR LS ", " CD ", " GET ", " PUT ", " RM ", " MKDIR ", " MV ", " CHMOD " and " STAT .
The transport is libssh2, so a transfer cannot be interrupted halfway. The host
key is checked against
.I ~/.ssh/known_hosts
and an unknown host is refused with its fingerprint;
.I ?insecure=1
is the way to say otherwise. Keys come from the agent and
.IR ~/.ssh/id_* ,
or from
.IR ?key= ,
and a password is asked for when neither works.
.PP
With no argument it opens the list of saved connections, where
.B enter
connects,
.B a
adds,
.B e
edits and
.B d
removes one.
Adding one asks which engine first and then for that engine's own fields - a host
and a database, a bucket and a region, a vhost, a file - and writes the target
itself. Editing takes a target apart again only when putting it back together
gives the same string; anything else stays the one field it was.
.PP
A SQLite file is opened through SQLite's own VFS: edits go straight to disk, the
database is never held in memory, and there is nothing to save.
.PP
Press
.B ?
inside the app for the whole key map, and
.B ctrl+k
for a command palette that finds a command by a few letters of its name and shows
the key that runs it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print what this build calls itself and exit. A release says its tag; a build from
a working tree says
.IR "from source" ,
because a version number from an unknown commit is worse than none.
.PP
There are no other options: everything else is done inside the app.
.SH KEYS
A few worth knowing before the first run.
.TP
.B ?
The key map.
.TP
.B ctrl+k
The command palette.
.TP
.B enter
Open what is under the cursor; in the object list, the table.
.TP
.B s
Write SQL.
.BR ctrl+s " runs it, " ctrl+p " walks the history."
.TP
.B S
The structure of the table: columns, indexes, foreign keys.
.TP
.B i
Insert a row.
.BR e " edits the one under the cursor, " d " deletes it."
.TP
.B W
Filter the rows.
.BR o " sorts by the column under the cursor."
.TP
.B R
Follow the table: read it again every couple of seconds and stay at the end of
it, which is where an append lands - a Kafka topic fills up on the screen instead
of being asked about again and again.
.B r
reads it once,
.B :follow
sets how often, and turning a page stops it.
.TP
.B C
Copy: then
.BR c " for the cell, " r " for the row, " p " for the page as CSV, " s
for the last statement. It goes through OSC 52, so it reaches the clipboard over
ssh and inside tmux.
.TP
.B ctrl+c
Give up on a statement that is taking too long. The connection stays usable.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.SH PASSWORDS
A password is asked for when the server wants one, and kept only where the
connection says: nowhere, the configuration file, or - on macOS - the login
keychain. An engine's own store works as it always did:
.BR PGPASSWORD ,
.IR ~/.pgpass ,
.IR ~/.my.cnf .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/krtek/connections
The saved connections, one
.RI \(dq name<TAB>target \(dq
line each, readable and editable by hand. A password kept in the file is on that
line and the file is mode 0600.
.TP
.IR ~/.aws/credentials ", " ~/.aws/config
Read for an S3 target that carries no key of its own, for the profile named with
.IR ?profile= ,
or
.BR default .
Neither file is written.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KRTEK_THEME
.BR light " or " dark
to settle the colours by hand. Without it the terminal is asked what its
background is.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the
.I krtek
configuration directory lives, if not
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.BR PGPASSWORD ", " PGSSLMODE ", ..."
Read by libpq, as usual.
.TP
.BR AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID ", " AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY ", " AWS_SESSION_TOKEN ", " AWS_REGION
Read for an S3 target that carries no key of its own, before
.I ~/.aws
is looked at.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit, including
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
The connection could not be made. The reason is on standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sqlite3 (1),
.BR psql (1),
.BR mysql (1),
.BR redis-cli (1),
.BR aws (1)
.SH BUGS
https://github.com/zales/krtek/issues
